<!-- formalization -->

**Definition (`InfoSys.join`).** When the union of two elements’ carriers is finitely consistent, their join is the deductive closure of that union.

**Theorem (`InfoSys.join.le`).** If both arguments approximate an upper bound $z$, then the join approximates $z$.

*Proof.* Antecedents drawn from the carrier union lie in $z$, so $z$ absorbs the entailments defining the join. □

**Theorem (`InfoSys.join.le_left`).** The left argument approximates the join.

*Proof.* Its carrier is contained in the union, hence in the deductive closure. □

**Theorem (`InfoSys.join.le_right`).** The right argument approximates the join.

*Proof.* Symmetric to the left projection. □

**Theorem (`InfoSys.join.exists_iff`).** Two elements have a common upper bound if and only if the union of their carriers is finitely consistent.

*Proof.* A common upper bound witnesses finite consistency of the union; conversely the join supplies a common upper bound by the two projections. □
